        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How We Chose These Folding Bikes</h2>
<p>
            We have not ridden or measured these bikes. They were chosen from makers' and retailers'
            listings, warranty terms and independent hands-on reviews, checked on 19 October 2026. Each pick
            had to fold, be on sale under its current name, and have its weight limit and resistance agree
            between the listing and at least one independent source. We favored bikes sold on Amazon and by
            brands with a way to reach them outside Amazon.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Recall checks.</strong> We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall
            database by brand for every maker on this page — Marcy and its company Impex, Exerpeutic,
            LEIKE and <a href="/xterra-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>XTERRA</a> — and for exercise and folding bikes as product types.
            None of the five bikes is under a recall. Impex Fitness recalled dumbbell handles in November
            2021, which does not cover a bike. The home exercise-bike recalls we found since 2015 are
            Peloton's: the original Peloton Bike (PL01) in May 2023 and the Original Series Bike+ (PL02) in
            November 2025, both for seat posts that could break; Johnson Health Tech also recalled commercial
            Matrix training cycles in January 2025. None is a folding bike, but if you are
            comparing a used Peloton with a new folding bike, check the model and serial number first.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What we left out, and why</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Marcy NS-654 and NS-653.</strong> The black Marcy folding bike that some reviewers
            tested is listed as NS-654 and marked "Retired" on Marcy's own store, so we carded the current
            NS-652 instead. Marcy's NS-653, with a high back seat, was in stock at $249.99, but Marcy lists a
            220 lb limit and we found no independent review of it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sunny Health &amp; Fitness X-Bikes.</strong> Sunny had its Foldable Magnetic Exercise
            X-Bike (SF-B223011) at $179.99 and its Smart Total Body X-Bike (SF-B224090), which Sunny lists
            with 14 magnetic levels and a 300 lb limit, at $274.99. We found no independent coverage of either
            to check Sunny's figures against.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Merach S15.</strong> BarBend picked it as its folding bike with an app, and Merach and
            BarBend agree on a 300 lb limit and 16 magnetic levels. But Merach's US store redirected its
            product page to a general bike collection when we checked, and Merach's and BarBend's dimensions
            disagree, so we could not confirm it as currently sold by the maker.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sharper Image Space Saving Stationary Bike.</strong> It folds smaller than anything here,
            but at $349.99 it has no handlebars or backrest, and BarBend's tester said it felt like a
            unicycle and would only use it on hardwood floors. <strong>PooBoo's folding bike</strong> is
            BarBend's budget pick, but BarBend reports a high step-over that shorter users struggled with.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="folded-footprint" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Folded Footprint: What "Space-Saving" Really Means</h2>
<p>
            A folding bike saves floor space, not height. When it folds, the X closes and the bike stands on
            its end, so the floor area shrinks to roughly that of a kitchen chair while the height goes up.
            That matters if you plan to store it under a desk, in a closet or under the stairs.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>XTERRA FB350:</strong> folds to about 20.5 by 20 inches of floor, according to Lowe's, BJ's and Garage Gym Reviews.</li>
            <li><strong>Exerpeutic Folding Magnetic Upright:</strong> 22 by 20 inches folded and 55.5 inches tall, by Garage Gym Reviews and <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-at-costco/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Costco</a>'s listing.</li>
            <li><strong>Marcy NS-653:</strong> neither Marcy nor the retailers we checked publish a folded size; it is 39 by 22 inches in use.</li>
            <li><strong>Marcy (reviewed version):</strong> Garage Gym Reviews gives 18.5 by 18.5 inches and 51.5 inches tall folded; BarBend gives 18.25 by 18.25 by 51.5 inches. Marcy does not publish a folded size for the NS-652.</li>
            <li><strong>LEIKE X Bike:</strong> BarBend gives 13 by 16 inches and 53 inches tall folded; we found no second source for the folded size.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            So plan on a space about 1.5 to 2 feet square and 4.5 feet tall for storage. Measure the closet
            or corner — including the height under a shelf or the slope of a staircase — before you buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>In use</strong>, the bikes take far more room: about 31 to 43 inches long and 18 to 22
            inches wide, plus space to get on and off. The Exerpeutic is 31 by 19 inches by Garage Gym Reviews'
            and Costco's figures; Marcy's NS-653 is 39 by 22 inches by Marcy's, Buydig's and Costco's
            figures. A bike with a reclining backrest is longer in use than a plain upright.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Folding is only useful if it is easy.</strong> Reviewers found the XTERRA's folding
            cumbersome and its wheels of little help, and BarBend's testers found the Merach S15's screw knob
            fiddly. A bike that is a chore to fold tends to stay unfolded, at which point you have bought a
            light, wobbly bike for no benefit. Check reviews for how the hinge and transport wheels work, and
            whether the bike will stand on its own when folded.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-limits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Weight Limits: Reading the Listing Claims</h2>
<p>
            Folding bike weight limits run from 220 lb to 330 lb on the listings we read, on frames that weigh
            37 to 42 lb. Those figures are the makers' or sellers' claims; none of the independent reviews we
            read loaded a bike to its limit. Garage Gym Reviews puts the category average at about 250 to 275
            lb, which is a useful benchmark: a figure well above it on a light frame deserves a second look.
          </p>
          <p>
            The picks show the range. XTERRA lists 225 lb for the FB350, and Top Ten Reviews called that the
            lowest in its comparison. Marcy lists 250 lb. Marcy lists 220 lb for the NS-653. The Exerpeutic is listed at 300 lb,
            and independent reviews and second retailers agree. The LEIKE is the instructive case: BarBend and
            Garage Gym Reviews' specification box both say 330 lb, while Garage Gym Reviews' own text says the
            bike can accommodate riders up to 300 lb. When a single source disagrees with itself, we leave the
            figure off the card.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What the limit means.</strong> A weight limit is the most the maker says the bike should
            carry in normal use, usually with some safety margin built in. Exceeding it voids most warranties,
            and on a folding bike the stress falls on the center hinge, the seat post and the welds around
            them. Our guide to <a href="/what-happens-if-you-exceed-treadmill-weight-limit/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what happens if you exceed a weight limit</a> explains the general principle. If you weigh within about 30 lb of a folding bike's limit, choose a
            heavier non-folding bike or a recumbent instead; the <a href="/best-exercise-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">exercise bikes</a> on our main
            roundup are rated to 300 to 350 lb on frames two to three times as heavy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="seat-comfort" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Seat Comfort and Fit</h2>
<p>
            The seat is where folding bikes win or lose their owners. Most have a wide, deeply padded saddle,
            much larger than a road or spin bike's, because riders sit upright with all their weight on it.
            Some add a backrest.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Upright X-bikes</strong> (Marcy, Exerpeutic): a large padded seat and no back support. Garage Gym Reviews found the Exerpeutic's seat much more comfortable than a typical indoor cycling saddle, though some Amazon reviewers found its size awkward. Neither seat adjusts fore and aft.</li>
            <li><strong>Seats with backrests</strong> (Marcy NS-653, LEIKE, XTERRA): the backrest takes load off the lower back and lets you lean back. The LEIKE seat reclines and the NS-653 has a tall backrest and seat handles, so both can be ridden more like a recumbent; reviewers liked the LEIKE's position for longer, gentler sessions.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Height and inseam</h3>
          <p>
            Fit ranges on folding bikes are narrower than on full-size bikes, and they matter. Marcy gives an
            inseam range of 28 to 33 inches, measured from seat to pedal, for the NS-652, and BarBend quotes a
            recommended rider height of 5 ft 1 in. to 6 ft 3 in. for the Marcy it tested. Garage Gym Reviews
            says the XTERRA's seat suits riders from about 4 ft 10 in. to 5 ft 10 in., and gives a maximum rider
            height of 6 ft 1 in. for the Exerpeutic. Garage Gym Reviews' 6 ft 3 in. reviewer fitted the LEIKE,
            which is why we suggest it for tall riders. If you are near either end of a range, measure your
            inseam in socks and ask the seller before you buy.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Set-up.</strong> With your heel on the pedal at the bottom of the stroke, your leg should
            be almost straight; riding normally on the ball of the foot then leaves a slight bend at the knee.
            A seat that is too low is the usual cause of sore knees on any bike, as our <a href="/treadmill-vs-exercise-bike/#knees" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill vs exercise bike</a> page explains. Use the pedal straps; feet sliding off is a common annoyance on budget bikes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="resistance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Resistance Levels: What Eight, Ten or Sixteen Mean</h2>
<p>
            Most folding bikes use a magnetic system adjusted by a knob with numbered steps. The Marcy and
            Exerpeutic have eight levels, the Marcy NS-653 eight, the LEIKE ten, and the Merach S15 we left out
            sixteen. The XTERRA has eight levels of manual tension resistance.
          </p>
          <p>
            The number of levels describes how finely you can adjust, not how hard the bike can get, and a
            level on one brand bears no relation to a level on another. What reviewers consistently report is
            that folding bikes top out lower than full-size bikes. Garage Gym Reviews' tester found the Marcy did
            not have enough resistance for her real workouts and uses it for warm-ups and cool-downs, and said
            the Exerpeutic's resistance may not challenge advanced exercisers.
          </p>
          <p>
            That is partly physics. Folding bikes have very small flywheels — retailer listings give 3.3 lb for
            the XTERRA's — so they carry little momentum, and the pedal stroke can feel less smooth at slow
            cadences than on a bike with a heavy wheel. It is also the trade-off for a 40 lb bike you can lift.
            For gentle cardio, recovery rides and pedaling while you watch television, eight levels are plenty.
            For intervals that leave you breathless, look at a full-size bike or, for short all-out efforts, an <a href="/best-air-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">air bike</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Measuring effort.</strong> Folding bike consoles show time, speed, distance and an estimated
            calorie count, and some read your pulse through hand sensors. Those readings are rough. A chest
            strap is far more reliable if heart rate matters to you; see our comparison of <a href="/chest-strap-vs-wrist-heart-rate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">chest straps and wrist heart rate</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stability" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Stability: Why Some Folding Bikes Wobble</h2>
<p>
            A folding bike stands on two narrow feet and weighs about 40 lb, and the center hinge that lets it
            fold is also a point that can flex. That is why "wobble" is the most common complaint in reviews of
            the category, and why the bikes that reviewers find steady stand out.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Steadiest in reviews:</strong> the Marcy. Garage Gym Reviews' testers noticed no uncomfortable shaking even pedaling hard at high resistance, and BarBend's tester found it stable "unlike some of the other folding bikes" they had tried.</li>
            <li><strong>Some wobble reported:</strong> the LEIKE, which BarBend and Garage Gym Reviews found slightly shaky when pedaling hard at low resistance, with screws needing retightening every couple of months; and, among bikes we first considered, FitNation's Flex Bike Ultra, which BarBend's tester called shaky.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>What helps.</strong> Ride on a hard, level floor or a firm mat rather than deep carpet; our <a href="/best-treadmill-mats/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">equipment mat guide</a> covers
            options, and a small mat suits a folding bike. Use the leveling caps on the feet if the bike has
            them. Check that the folding lock pin or knob is fully engaged before every ride. Retighten bolts
            after the first week and then every month or two. And sit and pedal smoothly rather than standing
            or rocking: folding bikes are not built for out-of-the-saddle efforts.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Noise.</strong> Magnetic folding bikes are among the quietest exercise machines; BarBend's
            LEIKE tester reported no complaints from downstairs neighbors and a partner using it during work
            calls. Squeaks usually come from loose bolts or the seat, not the resistance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buyers-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Folding Exercise Bike Buyer's Guide</h2>
<ol>
            <li><strong>Check the weight limit against the heaviest rider,</strong> with a margin, and prefer a figure that a reviewer or second retailer confirms.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the fit range</strong> — inseam if given, height if not — for the shortest and tallest riders.</li>
            <li><strong>Decide on the seat.</strong> Upright with a big saddle, or a backrest you can lean on. A backrest helps sore backs and longer rides; an upright bike is shorter in use.</li>
            <li><strong>Measure the storage spot,</strong> including height, against the folded dimensions.</li>
            <li><strong>Read about the hinge and wheels.</strong> If reviewers say folding is a chore, assume you will leave it unfolded.</li>
            <li><strong>Read the warranty.</strong> Garage Gym Reviews lists one year on the Exerpeutic's frame and 90 days on other parts; BarBend and Garage Gym Reviews give two years on the Marcy's parts; the NS-653's Amazon listing gives two years. These are short, which is normal for the price.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            <strong>Is a folding bike the right machine?</strong> For a small apartment, a guest room, an older
            rider who wants a gentle daily ride, or anyone who needs the floor back after a workout, it is
            hard to beat. For hard training, a heavy rider or a household of very different heights, a
            full-size bike will serve better and last longer. For more ways to fit cardio into a small home,
            see our guides to <a href="/best-compact-ellipticals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">compact ellipticals</a>, <a href="/best-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding treadmills</a> and <a href="/best-under-desk-ellipticals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk ellipticals</a>,
            or browse the <a href="/home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Home Gym hub</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Folding Exercise Bike</h2>
          <p>Folding bikes are cheap enough to buy on impulse, which is how these mistakes happen.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting the highest weight limit on the listing</h3>
              <p>Folding bike weight limits are sellers' claims on light frames, and even one review can give two different figures for the same bike. Choose a limit confirmed by a reviewer or second retailer, leave a margin, and buy a heavier bike if you are near the top of the range.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Measuring the floor but not the height</h3>
              <p>Folded bikes save floor space by standing on end, at 51 to 56 inches tall. A bike that folds to 20 inches square may still not fit under a shelf, a desk or the slope of a staircase. Measure the storage spot in all three directions.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Expecting a spin-bike workout</h3>
              <p>A 40 lb folding bike with a small flywheel will not feel like a studio bike, and reviewers say several top out too easy for hard training. Buy one for steady, moderate riding, and choose a full-size or air bike if you want intervals that leave you breathless.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring the hinge and loose bolts</h3>
              <p>The center hinge is a folding bike's weak point. Check that the lock pin or knob is fully engaged before every ride, retighten bolts after the first week and then every month or two, and ride on a firm, level floor. Most wobble complaints start with one of these.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">For most people, buy the Marcy Foldable Upright Exercise Bike</strong>:
            steady for a folding bike, eight magnetic levels, a 250 lb limit you can trust, and about $170.
            If you weigh more, the 300 lb Exerpeutic is the safer choice; if you want to lean back, the
            Marcy NS-653 or, for tall riders, the LEIKE X Bike.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before you order, check the weight limit against the heaviest rider with a margin, the fit range
            against the tallest and shortest, and the folded height against your storage spot. If you want
            harder training than a folding bike can give, our <a href="/best-exercise-bikes/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best exercise bikes</a> guide covers the full-size options.
          </p>
        </div>

