        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best folding exercise bike?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most people, the Marcy Foldable Upright Exercise Bike: eight magnetic resistance levels, a 250 lb limit that Marcy and two independent reviews agree on, and a frame reviewers found unusually steady for a folding bike, for about $170. For riders up to 300 lb, the Exerpeutic Folding Magnetic Upright Bike is the pick.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are folding exercise bikes any good?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For gentle to moderate riding in a small space, yes. They weigh about 40 lb, fold to roughly 1.5 to 2 feet square, and are quiet. Their resistance tops out lower than a full-size bike, their small flywheels feel less smooth, and some wobble, so they suit everyday cardio and warm-ups more than hard training.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much weight can a folding exercise bike hold?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Listings we read ran from 220 lb to 330 lb, and reviewers put the category average at about 250 to 275 lb. These are the sellers' claims on frames of about 40 lb. Prefer a limit that an independent reviewer or second retailer confirms, and leave a margin of 30 lb or more.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much space does a folding exercise bike take up?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              In use, about 31 to 43 inches long and 18 to 22 inches wide. Folded, about 17 to 22 inches on each side of the floor, but 51 to 56 inches tall, because the bike stands on its end. Measure the height of your storage spot as well as the floor.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a folding bike good for seniors?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can be, for gentle daily riding, and a model with a backrest such as Marcy's NS-653 adds support. But folding bikes are light and some wobble, and getting on and off an upright frame needs some balance. A recumbent bike with a step-through frame is often steadier for older riders. Ask a doctor about any health concerns.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Magnetic or manual resistance on a folding bike?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Magnetic resistance is quieter and smoother, because nothing rubs on the flywheel, and it is what most folding bikes now use. The XTERRA FB350 uses manual tension resistance, which is cheaper. On either kind, the number of levels describes how finely you can adjust, not how hard the bike gets.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
